.TH GIT-WSWEEP 1 "2026-07-09" "gitplus" "gitplus Manual"
.SH NAME
git-wsweep \- remove worktrees (and their branches) merged into the base
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git wsweep
[\fB\-f\fR|\fB\-\-force\fR]
[\fB\-b\fR|\fB\-\-base\fR \fINAME\fR]
[\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
[\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The worktree analogue of
.BR git-sweep (1).
Removes merged
.I worktrees
\(em the linked working directories \(em
.B and
deletes the underlying branch, same as running
.BR git-sweep (1)
right afterward \(em you are almost always done with both at once. A
detached-HEAD worktree has no branch, so only the worktree goes.
.PP
Like git-sweep, it fetches first and judges "merged" against the base's remote
tip
.RI ( origin/<base> ),
so it works without pulling the base locally and from any worktree.
.PP
Safe by default: a worktree (and its branch) is removed only when its HEAD is
already an ancestor of the base's remote tip (verified with
.BR "git merge-base \-\-is-ancestor" ),
so no unmerged work is lost. Reachability is judged by the worktree's HEAD
commit, so both branch-backed and detached worktrees are handled. The branch
delete uses
.B \-D
rather than
.B \-d
on purpose, same reasoning as
.BR git-sweep (1):
.B \-d
rechecks mergedness against your
.I current
branch, which wrongly refuses a branch merged upstream when your local base is
stale; the merge-base check above is done instead, and trusted. The main
worktree (always listed first) and the worktree you are currently in are never
touched or deleted.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Remove every worktree except the main one and the current one, regardless of
merge status, with
.BR "git worktree remove \-\-force" .
This also drops worktrees that have uncommitted changes, discarding those
changes.
.B Branches are not deleted in force mode
\(em that would compound two separately destructive actions into one flag; run
.B git sweep \-f
afterward if you want those gone too.
.B "It discards uncommitted work"
\(em preview with
.B \-n
first.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-base " " \fINAME\fR
Base branch to compare against instead of origin's default.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
List what would be removed without changing anything.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B git wsweep
Remove worktrees (and their branches) whose HEAD is merged into the base (safe).
.TP
.B git wsweep \-f \-n
Preview a force sweep of every worktree except main and current.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git-sweep (1),
.BR git-sync (1),
.BR git-pr (1),
.BR git-done (1),
.BR git-worktree (1)
